A function with an explicit typed error path adds one final return marked with `!`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    parse_number |text String| -> I32, Error!:
        if text is "":
            return! Error("Missing number")
        ;

        return 42
    ;
]

Normal `return` sends success values.

`return!` sends one error value.

The caller must handle that error by propagating it or catching it.

Checked numeric work has a separate implicit built-in failure path. Under the
accepted contract, `Error!` materialises that failure for callers. A custom error
slot needs local recovery or explicit mapping with `catch` and `return!`.

Private functions without an error slot may propagate implicit failure inside
their module. Exported callables must declare `Error!` or handle that failure
locally. See @../progress (Progress) for current compiler support.
